{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And He spoke, etc. — all the words, etc.</b> The reason for the mention of the name Elohim at the giving of the Torah is to say that the Torah was given both from the attribute of judgment and from the attribute of mercy — the attribute of judgment: <b>וַיְדַבֵּר אֱלֹהִים</b> (and Elohim spoke); the attribute of mercy: <b>אָנֹכִי ה׳</b> (I am YHWH). Further, He made known the sod of His saying (I Kings 18:39) ״ה׳ הוּא הָאֱלֹהִים״ — for He spoke Elohim yet said 'I am YHWH,' and this is what we unify twice in love in the verse ״שְׁמַע יִשְׂרָאֵל ה׳ אֱלֹהֵינוּ ה׳ אֶחָד״." "<b>Further,</b> it may be explained along the lines of what Rav Ktina said (Menachot 41a) ״עַנְשִׁיתוּ אַעֲשֵׂה״ etc. — up to here, meaning that the verse does not impose punishment for a positive commandment. Therefore HaShem said that when HaShem brings judgment, He imposes punishment for all the words, even for the positive commandments inscribed in the Ten Commandments: His faithful trust, blessed be He — for it is the foundation of everything — inscribed at the beginning of the word of HaShem: ״אָנֹכִי״ etc.; Sabbath; honoring father and mother. Upon these, even though they are positive commandments, there is an Elohim who judges on earth, and this is what is meant by <b>וַיְדַבֵּר אֱלֹהִים</b> — which is the aspect of judgment — <b>אֵת כָּל הַדְּבָרִים</b> (all the words), whether positive commandments or prohibitions." "<b>Further,</b> it intends by saying <b>אֵת כָּל הַדְּבָרִים</b> (all the words) to say that there is no desire in receiving the Torah unless one receives the entire Torah, and anyone who accepts the Torah upon himself except for one commandment has no Torah (Bekhorot 30a)." "<b>Further,</b> it intends according to what I prefaced in the portion of Bereishit: that when YHWH speaks through the supernal power known by the name Elohim, He speaks wondrous things that the mouth cannot speak — that is to say, He speaks complete words and likewise many words in a single utterance, as He did in the creation of the world. So too on the day of the giving of the Torah, He spoke through that supernal power <b>אֵת כָּל הַדְּבָרִים</b> (all the words) in a single utterance." "And His saying the word <b>לֵאמֹר</b> (saying) will be explained by raising a point that must be raised regarding the text of the Ten Commandments: why did the verse change the formulation from commandment to commandment? For at first He began to speak in this order: <b>אָנֹכִי ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ</b>, <b>הוֹצֵאתִיךָ</b>, <b>עַל פָּנַי</b> — for YHWH Himself is speaking about Himself, blessed be His name. And afterward He went forth to speak in a different manner: <b>אֶת שֵׁם ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ</b>, <b>לֹא יְנַקֶּה ה׳</b>, <b>שַׁבָּת לַה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ</b>, <b>כִּי שֵׁשֶׁת יָמִים עָשָׂה ה׳</b> — whereas He should have concluded according to the manner in which He began, in this way: ״לֹא תִשָּׂא אֶת שְׁמִי, כִּי לֹא אֲנַקֶּה, כִּי שֵׁשֶׁת יָמִים עָשִׂיתִי״ etc." "<b>Thus</b> it may be explained according to the statement of the Sages of blessed memory (Makkot 24a): ״אָנֹכִי״ and ״לֹא יִהְיֶה לְךָ״ — from the mouth of the Almighty we heard them, meaning, through the overwhelming power of the utterance it is understood that when HaShem spoke the Ten Commandments in a single utterance, as we have written nearby, the ear is unable to hear all of its mighty force. And when He caused Israel to hear them, they grasped hearing two commandments among them, which are the utterance ״אָנֹכִי״ etc. and the utterance ״לֹא יִהְיֶה לְךָ״, and their souls departed, as it says (Song of Songs 5:6): ״נַפְשִׁי יָצְאָה בְדַבְּרוֹ״, and they could no longer comprehend. And the rest of the commandments were hewn into flames of fire from His voice, blessed be He, and stood arrayed upon Mount Sinai until the soul of Israel returned to the cessation of life. The commandments — which are the voices of the Mighty One, blessed be He — came and spoke to each and every one of Israel. And therefore you will find that ״אָנֹכִי״ and ״לֹא יִהְיֶה לְךָ״ were engraved in the Torah in the very order that HaShem said them: <b>״אָנֹכִי ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ אֲשֶׁר הוֹצֵאתִיךָ״</b>, for the Divine in His glory spoke directly to Israel on His own behalf, blessed be He, and likewise in the commandment <b>״לֹא יִהְיֶה לְךָ״</b> He too speaks in this same order. But from there onward His voice, blessed be He, speaks through His angel, as it says <b>״שַׁבָּת לַה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ״</b>, <b>״כִּי שֵׁשֶׁת יָמִים עָשָׂה ה׳״</b>, <b>״עַל כֵּן בֵּרַךְ ה׳״</b> etc., and so all of them, for the voice cannot say ״שַׁבָּת לִי״ etc., ״עָשִׂיתִי אֶת הַשָּׁמַיִם״ etc., ״וְנָתַתִּי״ etc., since it is only His angel, blessed be He — and understand, and may it be sweet to your palate." "And although the Sages of blessed memory said (Makkot 23b) that Moses said them to Israel, it is possible that Moses had the capacity and heard all of them, and when the voices came to speak to Israel, the voice of Moses was also speaking. Or perhaps to this the verse alludes in saying (Exodus 19:19): ״מֹשֶׁה יְדַבֵּר וְהָאֱלֹהִים יַעֲנֶנּוּ בְקוֹל״. And you will find that the Sages of blessed memory said (Shir HaShirim Rabbah 1:2) that each and every utterance stood over each and every one of Israel and said to him: ״תְּקַבְּלֵנִי עָלֶיךָ״, and he would say to him ״הֵן״, and he would embrace him and kiss him and ascend and crown himself upon his head. And according to this, the saying of <b>לֵאמֹר</b> is resolved in this manner: <b>וַיְדַבֵּר אֱלֹהִים אֵת כָּל</b> etc. — meaning, through the overwhelming power of Elohim He spoke all of them in a single utterance, and also invested power in the commandments so that they themselves would be spoken to Israel whenever necessary, as indeed came to pass, for when Israel could not hear all of them from the Almighty's power, they [the commandments] spoke to them as we have written." "<b>Further</b>, it may intend in the saying of <b>לֵאמֹר</b>, that there is in this order in which the commandments were spoken a sublimity and exaltation for Israel, along the lines of what it says (Deuteronomy 26:18): ״וַה׳ הֶאֱמִירְךָ״ etc., for many benefits were drawn from the fact that their impurity (זוּהֲמָה) was severed, and this is the foundation of the acquisition of holiness within them forever." "<b>Further</b>, the verse may be explained according to the statement of the Sages of blessed memory (Tanchuma, Toldot) that the Holy One, blessed be He, does not unify His name upon the creatures during the life of their vitality, because of (Job 15:15): ״הֵן בִּקְדוֹשָׁיו לֹא יַאֲמִין״, and therefore He did not unify His name upon Abraham to say of him ״אֱלֹהֶיךָ״ except after his death. And this is what it says: <b>וַיְדַבֵּר אֱלֹהִים אֵת כָּל הַדְּבָרִים הָאֵלֶּה</b> — meaning, through the overwhelming power of the utterance, things which the ear cannot hear, as explained. And the reason is <b>לֵאמֹר</b> to them: ״אָנֹכִי ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ״ — meaning, to unify His name upon each and every one of them, to say to him ״אֱלֹהֶיךָ״; therefore He caused them to hear His voice in order to chastise them, to separate the impurity (זוּהֲמָא) from them, and thereby they would become worthy to have this said to them." "And according to the words of our Sages of blessed memory (Shabbat 88b) who said that their souls departed, you find that when He unified His name upon them it was at the time of the departure of the soul — then He said to each of them ״אֱלֹהֶיךָ״. And although they were alive and also heard ״לֹא יִהְיֶה לְךָ״ etc., no delay may be attributed to it, for HaShem said everything in a single utterance, and not even one letter preceded its fellow." "Further, His saying <b>לֵאמֹר</b> intends what the Sages, z״l, said (Zohar II:93a) — that the entire Torah is alluded to within the Ten Commandments. Thus His saying <b>לֵאמֹר</b> means that these words contain other utterances, included and alluded to within them. And I have seen fit to give a good reason for HaShem's causing Israel to hear two commandments directly — ״אָנֹכִי״ and ״לֹא יִהְיֶה לְךָ״ — for they are two roots for the two general categories of commandments: positive commandments and negative commandments. <b>אָנֹכִי</b> is the core and root of all positive commandments; <b>לֹא יִהְיֶה לְךָ</b> etc. is the core and root of all negative commandments. Therefore HaShem implanted within them, from the mouth of El Elyon, the roots of the two foundational principles of the commandments, so that through this the Torah shall never be uprooted from our seed forever and ever."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :register {:flags {5 ["the Almighty"], 6 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:31:15.594905909Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}